@article{MUSSELMAN2025e43,
 abstract = {Objectives
To investigate the effects of a photovoice intervention on falls self-efficacy and concern about falling in individuals with chronic spinal cord injury (SCI). Secondary objectives included examining the effects of a photovoice intervention on participation levels and quality of life in individuals with chronic SCI. We hypothesized that after photovoice, falls self-efficacy, participation and quality of life would improve, whereas concern about falling would decrease.
Design
Convergent mixed methods design. A pre-post design was used for the quantitative strand, with questionnaires administered preintervention, immediately postintervention and 3 months postintervention. A qualitative descriptive design was used for the qualitative strand, with semistructured interviews occurring 3 months postintervention. Integration occurred at the level of data analysis/interpretation.
Setting
Virtual, community setting in Canada and the United States.
Participants
Volunteer sample of 34 adults with chronic SCI. Seventeen participants used a wheelchair (13 men, 4 women, aged 49.0±11.5y) and 17 participants ambulated (6 men, 11 women, aged 63.6±10.5y).
Interventions
The 6-week photovoice intervention involved 2 photo-assignments, 2 individual interviews with a peer mentor and researcher, and 4 group meetings (8-9 participants/group, grouped by mobility status). Participants used photographs and dialogue to identify and share strategies they used to prevent falling.
Main Outcome Measures
The primary outcome measures were the Activities-specific Balance Confidence Scale and Falls Efficacy Scale-International for participants who ambulated, and the Wheelchair Use Confidence Scale and Spinal Cord Injury Falls Concern Scale for those who used a wheelchair. Secondary measures included the Impact on Participation and Autonomy Questionnaire and Life Satisfaction Questionnaire-9. Scores were compared over time using a repeated-measures analysis of variance or Friedman's test. Qualitative data were analyzed using a reflexive thematic analysis. Quantitative and qualitative data were merged via joint display.
Results
There were no changes in the primary measures over time for both groups (P≥.109). Participation scores were significantly greater 3 months postintervention in the ambulatory group (P≤.028). There were no significant changes over time in other secondary measures (P≥.120). Concordant findings were observed between the quantitative and qualitative data. Although some participants stated their falls self-efficacy and participation improved, others did not. Participants indicated the intervention lacked relevancy in chronic SCI; they have “been through almost every scenario possible” and know how to manage their fall risk.
Conclusions
Ambulatory individuals with chronic SCI showed increased participation levels after a photovoice intervention, but no change in falls self-efficacy, concern about falling or quality of life. Photovoice did not impact the outcomes of wheelchair users with SCI.
Disclosures
none.},
 author = {Kristin Musselman and James Sessford and Anita Kaiser and Katherine Chan and Oda Al-anizi and Hardeep Singh and Sarah Munce},
